/**
\defgroup Animation Animation
\brief Sprite playback, poses, procedural animation, constraints, and custom artwork.

\section animation_start Choosing an API

| Task | C++ entry points |
| --- | --- |
| Program object movement, rotation, or size changes | pdg::Animated (typed fluent interface), inherited by pdg::Sprite, pdg::SpriteLayer, and pdg::Part |
| Share an animation interface across different owners | pdg::AnimatedBase |
| Share appearance-animation state across different owners | pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase |
| Animate drawing colors, opacity, gradients, text size or transforms | pdg::AnimatedAttributes |
| Create an independently animated part with optional bone binding | pdg::Sprite::createPart(), pdg::Part |
| Play image frames | pdg::Sprite::addFramesImage(), pdg::Sprite::startFrameAnimation(), pdg::Sprite::stopFrameAnimation() |
| Play and blend Spriter clips | pdg::Sprite::startAnimation(), pdg::Sprite::blendToAnimation(), pdg::Sprite::pauseAnimation(), pdg::Sprite::resumeAnimation() |
| Enable pose evaluation and inspect a rig | pdg::Sprite::enableAnimationPose(), pdg::Sprite::getAnimationRig(), pdg::Sprite::getAnimationRigError() |
| Seek, sample, or transition to a clip position | pdg::Sprite::seekAnimation(), pdg::Sprite::sampleAnimationPose(), pdg::Sprite::transitionToAnimation() |
| Inspect or override bone and binding transforms | pdg::Sprite::getAnimationPose(), pdg::Sprite::getAnimationBoneTransform(), pdg::Sprite::getAnimationBindingTransform(), pdg::Sprite::setAnimationBoneTransform() |
| Add procedural pose changes or inverse kinematics | pdg::Sprite::addAnimationModifier(), pdg::Sprite::addAnimationIK(), pdg::Sprite::setAnimationSource(), \ref AnimationModifiers |
| Smooth targets or lock contacts to world/platform positions | \ref AnimationTargets |
| Drive bones with physics and recover to animation | pdg::Sprite::setupAnimationPhysics(), pdg::Sprite::disableAnimationPhysics(), \ref AnimationPhysics |
| Attach custom artwork or display rig diagnostics | pdg::Sprite::addAnimationDrawable(), pdg::Sprite::setAnimationDebugDraw(), \ref AnimationDrawing |
| Manage authored appearances and attachments | pdg::Sprite::applyCharacterMap(), pdg::Sprite::attachSprite(), pdg::Sprite::getAttachPoint() |
| Emit sparks, smoke and trails | pdg::Particle, pdg::ParticleEmitter, \ref ParticleEffects |
| Control animation for a layer | pdg::SpriteLayer::startAnimations(), pdg::SpriteLayer::stopAnimations() |
| Receive animation notifications | pdg::SpriteAnimateInfo, pdg::SpriteTriggerEventInfo, pdg::SpriteLayerInfo |
| Extend object motion or select easing curves | pdg::IAnimationHelper, \ref AnimationEasing |

\section animation_programming Programmed transforms and schedule controls

setScale(x,y) (or a uniform setScale(factor)) sets independent dimensionless scale;
changeScaleTo(x,y,seconds,easing) transitions it. getScale() returns a fresh Offset.
Logical width/height, resizeTo(), grow() and stretch() retain their size meanings.
Negative scale reflects an axis, and flip flags combine with its sign. Zero scale
is valid for display; inverse coordinate queries reject singular transforms.
Sprites use this same API for both image and Spriter artwork. Imported asset
normalization is private; Animated scale and ancestor transforms each apply once.

setGrowing(rate) and setStretching(xRate,yRate) immediately set constant logical
size changes per second. changeGrowingTo()/changeGrowingBy() and
changeStretchingTo()/changeStretchingBy() transition these rates over explicit
seconds. The final rate continues after the transition. getStretching() returns
the current two rates as an Offset. Size setters preserve growth rates;
stopGrowing()/stopStretching() cancel growth-rate and scheduled size changes.
These rates are programmed animation, without physical damping or forces.

pauseSchedule()/resumeSchedule() freeze/resume scheduled transitions and their delays.
They do not stop helpers, constant movement/spin/growth, Sprite clips or physics.
A paused rate transition holds its current rate while that motion continues.
cancelSchedule() discards scheduled targets and the pending wait, holding sampled
values and preserving the paused state. It emits no completion notification.
hasScheduledAnimations() includes delayed and paused transitions; isSchedulePaused() reports
the independent clock state. AnimatedAttributes includes appearance and frame
tracks in these controls.

Sprite snapshots preserve scale, scale/growth transitions, pause state and the
pending wait in animation record revision 3. Layer update snapshots also preserve active tween tracks, directed rotation,
rate transitions and pending waits. Standalone Animated records preserve its
transform, rates and tween state; subclass artwork/appearance/controller records
are separate. Full Layer graph reconstruction remains incomplete. Rect-returning
Layer conversions use conservative axis-aligned bounds when independent scale or
reflection is active; use the Quad overloads for exact corners, including shear.
Explicit collision radii, body mass and inertia are unchanged by visual scale;
Sprite bounding-box shapes follow scaled dimensions.

\section animation_availability Build capabilities

Spriter playback and the Sprite pose, modifier, IK, physics, and drawing entry
points require `PDG_SPRITER_SUPPORT`. Enable pose evaluation with
pdg::Sprite::enableAnimationPose() before using the pose-based Sprite extensions.
Physics simulation additionally requires `PDG_USE_CHIPMUNK_PHYSICS`; query
pdg::Sprite::supportsAnimationPhysics() for build support. Drawing replay requires
a GUI-capable build; query pdg::Sprite::isAnimationDrawingSupported(). Rig and
pose data, pose modifiers, and target helpers can be used without rendering.

The C++ reference includes the optional Spriter and Chipmunk interfaces so they
remain discoverable. Their presence in these pages does not enable them in an
application build.

\section animation_events Animation events

| Notification | Event type and payload |
| --- | --- |
| Authored Spriter eventline crossing | pdg::eventType_SpriteTriggerEvent with pdg::SpriteTriggerEventInfo; enable with pdg::Sprite::enableSpriterEvents(true) |
| Blend or independent transition completion | pdg::eventType_SpriteAnimate with pdg::SpriteAnimateInfo and action pdg::Sprite::action_AnimationBlendComplete |

The authored-trigger payload is listed under Events, Animation, and Sprites.
Use `triggerName` to identify its authored track. `timeSeconds` is relative to clip
start; `offsetSeconds` is relative to the current update. The inherited `action`
field is not used to identify triggers. Subscribe to the trigger event type and
inspect its authored name.
Blend completion covers both pdg::Sprite::blendToAnimation() and
pdg::Sprite::transitionToAnimation(), independently of authored-trigger enablement.

\section animation_timing Animation timing

**Floating-point seconds are the current animation timing convention.** For
example, pass `0.25` for a quarter-second blend, sample time, transition duration,
contact-release duration, or physics-recovery duration. Modifier callbacks receive
elapsed seconds in pdg::AnimationModifierContext::deltaSeconds.

Timed Animated operations, wait(), sprite fades, layer fades and camera zoom,
IAnimationHelper::animate(), and built-in/custom EasingFunc callbacks now use
floating-point seconds. Physical forces and velocities belong to PhysicsBody;
Animated contains only programmed transform changes.

The remaining millisecond boundaries are:

| Existing API or field | Current time units |
| --- | --- |
| pdg::SpriteLayer::animateLayer() | `ms_delta msElapsed`; this layer scheduler entry point still takes milliseconds and converts to seconds for Animated stepping |
| pdg::SpriteLayerInfo::millisec | Integer millisecond timestamp |
| pdg::SpriteTriggerEventInfo::frameTime | Compatibility integer millisecond field; use `timeSeconds` for authored clip time and `offsetSeconds` for the occurrence offset within the update |
| pdg::TimerManager::startTimer() and general event timestamps | Milliseconds; convert elapsed intervals explicitly when driving animation |

Do not infer units from a number's magnitude. When migrating an older caller of
a now-seconds API, convert its millisecond duration by dividing by `1000.0`.

Frame indices and counts are integers, `startFrameAnimation()` takes frames per
second, and animation/blend progress is a dimensionless fraction. These are not
time-duration arguments. Angles in pose transforms are radians; fixed choices
such as pdg::AnimationSpace use integer enum constants. Clip, bone, and slot names
remain strings.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationPoses Rigs, poses, and transforms
\ingroup Animation
\brief Rig structure, owned pose snapshots, coordinate spaces, and authored metadata.

pdg::AnimationRig stores immutable bones, sockets, and bindings. A
pdg::AnimationPose owns its mutable transforms and shares the rig; copying a pose
does not share mutable transforms. pdg::AnimationTransform describes translation,
rotation, scale, and opacity. pdg::AnimationSpace selects local, rig, or world
coordinates; world coordinates belong to the Sprite's owning layer.

pdg::AnimationMetadata retains authored variables and tags. Bone, socket, and
binding IDs are scoped to their rig instance and revision.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationModifiers Pose modifiers and inverse kinematics
\ingroup Animation
\brief Ordered pose callbacks, animation sources, and two-bone IK.

pdg::AnimationPipeline evaluates callbacks in pdg::AnimationModifierStage order.
A callback receives a pdg::AnimationPoseView and pdg::AnimationModifierContext;
the view expires when the callback returns. Call pdg::AnimationPoseView::copy()
to retain an owned snapshot. Select clip, reference, or procedural input with
pdg::AnimationSource.

pdg::AnimationTwoBoneIK describes a two-bone chain and its target. Use
pdg::solveAnimationTwoBoneIK() for an owned pose, or register a constraint with
pdg::Sprite::addAnimationIK(). pdg::AnimationIKResult reports reach and constraint
limits. Zero length fields use rig-declared lengths, or current child offsets
when the rig declares none; this follows animated joint offsets without using
visual bone widths. Explicit/declared lengths must match the offsets. Nonsingular
nonuniform scales and reflections are supported under PDG's component-transform
composition. Check pdg::Sprite::getAnimationModifierError() for callback failures.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationTargets Spring and contact targets
\ingroup Animation
\brief Spring integration, recoil impulses, and world or platform contact locks.

pdg::AnimationSpringTarget updates a position and velocity toward a target and
accepts recoil impulses. pdg::AnimationContactTarget locks a contact in world or
platform coordinates and releases it over a duration in seconds. Their snapshots
are pdg::AnimationTargetState and pdg::AnimationContactState.
Use pdg::animationHasTag() to query authored pose tags when deciding contact state.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationPhysics Animation physics
\ingroup Animation Physics
\brief Bone bodies, joints, simulation state, and recovery to authored animation.

pdg::AnimationPhysicsDefinition contains pdg::AnimationPhysicsBody and
pdg::AnimationPhysicsJoint entries. pdg::AnimationPhysicsBodyMode selects dynamic
or kinematic bodies; pdg::AnimationPhysicsRootMode selects fixed or following
roots. pdg::PhysicsBodyState reports positions and velocities through each mapped Part's physics property.

Use the Sprite physics methods for integration with its layer. The native
pdg::AnimationPhysicsRig provides direct integration with a Chipmunk space when
`PDG_USE_CHIPMUNK_PHYSICS` is enabled. Recovery durations are seconds, angular
values are radians, and velocities are per second.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationDrawing Animation artwork and diagnostics
\ingroup Animation Graphics
\brief Shared Drawing artwork attached to the evaluated pose and authored slots.

pdg::Sprite::addAnimationDrawable() accepts persistent pdg::Drawing artwork or a
synchronous pdg::AnimationDrawings::Callback returning a shared Drawing handle.
Returning an empty handle skips drawing for that invocation. Artwork shares
editable Drawing contents, including edits through pdg::ElementRef.

pdg::AnimationDrawableOptions selects a bone, slot placement, order, stroke space,
and optional bounds. Use pdg::AnimationDrawPlacement and pdg::AnimationStrokeSpace
constants. pdg::AnimationDrawingContext provides the invocation's transform and
an owned pose copy; the context expires when the callback returns.

pdg::AnimationDrawBounds describes bounds used for culling. Query
pdg::Sprite::getAnimationDrawableError() for callback failures. Use
pdg::AnimationDebugDraw flags with pdg::Sprite::setAnimationDebugDraw() to display
bones, sockets, and collision boxes.
*/

/**
\defgroup AnimationEasing Easing functions
\ingroup Animation
\brief Built-in interpolation curves and the easing callback signature.

pdg::EasingFunc describes a value over time; pdg::linearTween() and the ease-in,
ease-out, and ease-in-out families supply built-in curves. Elapsed time and
duration are floating-point seconds for both built-in and custom callbacks;
see \ref animation_timing.
*/

/** \class pdg::Animated
 * \ingroup Animation
 * \brief Base class for programmed movement, rotation, and resizing over time.
 */
/** \class pdg::Sprite
 * \ingroup Animation
 * \brief Animated image frames, Spriter playback, and pose-based animation extensions.
 */
/** \class pdg::SpriteLayer
 * \ingroup Animation
 * \brief Collection of sprites with shared animation control and layer transforms.
 */
/** \class pdg::IAnimationHelper
 * \ingroup Animation
 * \brief Callback interface for extending an Animated object's motion.
 */
/** \class pdg::Drawing
 * \ingroup AnimationDrawing Graphics
 * \brief Editable artwork shared by animation attachments and other drawing users.
 */
/** \class pdg::ElementRef
 * \ingroup AnimationDrawing Graphics
 * \brief Handle for editing an element in shared Drawing artwork.
 */
